%description:

RFC 5681 — the initial congestion window is at most the bound of the segment-size table, and
the handshake does not enlarge it.

Checks RFC5681-IW-1 (must, as an upper bound) and RFC5681-IW-2 (must not) from
doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc5681/catalog.md. The English procedure is in
doc/project/evidence/protocol/tcp/checks/congestion-control.md#initial-window; the program
steps below map onto its expected observations. Mapping of the plain mockup: host A = host1,
host B = host2.

This is the first test of the TCP suite that reads a control variable instead of a segment.
The congestion window never travels on the link: it is a limit the sender keeps. The tester
observes it through the `cwnd` scalar signal, which the ini names in `stateSignals`.

The segment size here is 536 bytes, the default of the model and the value RFC 1122 gives
for a host that learns nothing else. 536 falls in the lowest band of the table, so the bound
is 4 * 536 = 2144 bytes.

One property of the model decides the shape of this test. The window is published only when
the algorithm changes it, not when the connection sets it up, so the first value the signal
carries arrives with the first acknowledgment at about t=0.2. "The initial window" is
therefore observable as the first publication and not before it. The first step closes just
after that instant, at t=0.2002, and the second step proves that a publication happened at
all, so that the first step cannot pass on a model that stays silent.

The bound governs the initial window alone. After the transfer starts, slow start grows the
window past it, which is what the same section of RFC 5681 requires.

What this test cannot establish is worth stating. The framework matches the first event that
satisfies a pattern, and it cannot say "the first publication of this signal satisfies P".
The first step therefore forbids an oversized value in a time window rather than at an
ordinal position, and it depends on the scenario times to place that window. A framework
that could bind the first publication would state the rule directly. See the pass notes.

Expected result: PASS. The bound is an upper bound, so a smaller window conforms.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

// RFC 5681 §3.1, the lowest band of the table: SMSS <= 1095 gives IW <= 4 * SMSS.
const long SMSS = 536;
const long IW_BOUND = 4 * SMSS;   // 2144 bytes

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc5681_initial_window")
        // Observations 1 and 2 together. The window is published for the first time when the
        // transfer starts, so "the initial window" is the first value the signal carries.
        // This step forbids a value above the bound up to and including that first
        // publication, which is what the two statements demand: the table bounds the initial
        // window, and the handshake does not enlarge it.
        .never(on("host1.tcp").signal("cwnd")
                   .isAtLeast(IW_BOUND + 1)
                   .describe("a congestion window above the bound, up to the first publication")
                   .within(0.2002))
        // The signal is alive. Without this step the one above would pass on a model that
        // never publishes the window at all. The step puts no bound on the value, because
        // by this point the transfer has started and slow start is growing the window,
        // which RFC 5681 requires.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("cwnd")
                  .describe("a further publication of host A's congestion window")
                  .within(0.5));
}

%activity:
EV << "rfc5681_initial_window compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// The plain mockup: host A -- link L -- host B.
network Rfc5681IwNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        host1: StandardHost { @display("p=300,100"); }
        host2: StandardHost { @display("p=500,100"); }
    connections:
        host1.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> host2.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = Rfc5681IwNet
sim-time-limit = 2s
seed-set = 0

# Static ARP keeps the wire free of ARP request/reply noise.
**.ipv4.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"

# The congestion window is not a field of a segment, so the tester must be told to observe
# the scalar signal that carries it.
*.protocolTester.stateSignals = "cwnd"

# The segment size of the check. 536 is the lowest band of the table of RFC 5681 §3.1.
**.tcp.mss = 536

# The scenario constants of checks.md#common-mockups. Host B only receives.
*.host1.numApps = 1
*.host1.app[0].typename = "TcpSessionApp"
*.host1.app[0].connectAddress = "host2"
*.host1.app[0].connectPort = 1000
*.host1.app[0].tOpen = 0.1s
*.host1.app[0].tSend = 0.2s
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 20000B
*.host1.app[0].tClose = 1.5s
*.host2.numApps = 1
*.host2.app[0].typename = "TcpEchoApp"
*.host2.app[0].localPort = 1000
*.host2.app[0].echoFactor = 0

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc5681_initial_window: PASS
